{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_apwq2rggmn2zfl5q7u2bsk6t22ouu43yh4iy5whdzoczvkz6tqpa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Translation complete and verified.\n\n## The library\n\n`c_src/` is a single-module library (`CMakeLists.txt` globs `src/long.c` into `liblong.so`). The C `.so` exports exactly three public symbols; the Rust `.so` now exports the same three, with identical types and sizes:\n\n| Symbol | Kind | C | Rust |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `array` | `B` (bss object, 0x100000 bytes) | ✅ | ✅ |\n| `long_exec` | `T` `void(unsigned)` | ✅ | ✅ |\n| `perform_expensive_operations` | `T` `void()` | ✅ | ✅ |\n\n`comm -23` of the two `nm -D --defined-only` lists is **empty** in both directions — no missing symbols, no extras. No namespace-renaming macros exist in `include/long.h`.\n\n## Files written\n- `translation/Cargo.toml` — `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]`, release profile with LTO.\n- `translation/src/lib.rs` — full translation.\n\n## Fidelity decisions\n- **`srand`/`rand`/`printf` are called through `extern \"C\"` into glibc**, not reimplemented. This is required for byte-identical output: the array is seeded from glibc's exact PRNG sequence, and the single output line goes through the same stdio stream/buffering as the C version.\n- **Signed overflow reproduced, not \"fixed.\"** `x*3+7` and `x-(x<<1)` are UB in C but wrap on x86; I used `wrapping_mul`/`wrapping_add`/`wrapping_sub` and an unsigned-cast left shift so `INT_MIN` and friends behave exactly as gcc's codegen does. `x>>3` is Rust's arithmetic shift (matches gcc); `x/2 + x%7` uses Rust's truncating `/` and `%` (matches C).\n- **`array` is a real `.bss` global** exposed via `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub static mut`, accessed internally through `&raw mut` + a slice so no `static_mut_refs` UB-adjacent references are created.\n\n## Verification performed\n1. Built the C library with CMake; diffed symbol lists — clean.\n2. Differential harness linking the *same* C driver against each `.so` in turn, exercising `array` + `perform_expensive_operations` directly with an edge-case vector (`0, ±1, ±2, ±7, ±3, INT_MAX, INT_MIN, INT_MAX-1, INT_MIN+1, ±2^30`) plus three rand-seeded rounds over seeds `0, 1, 12345, 4294967295`, comparing per-element values, xor, and sum → **byte-identical**.\n3. Full `long_exec` end-to-end (all 2000 iterations, ~5.2×10¹⁰ inner steps) for seeds `0, 42, 4294967295` → **byte-identical** (`42032659`, `430392287`, `494145113`).\n\nOne performance note: my first straightforward version was ~3.5× slower than the C, because gcc applies *outer-loop* vectorisation here (SSE2, 4 elements at a time) and LLVM does not. Since each element is transformed completely independently, I restructured `perform_expensive_operations` to run the 100-step loop over batches of 8 neighbours via `chunks_exact_mut`, which LLVM SLP-vectorises. Same values, and it's now faster than the C build (0.12s vs 0.18s on the 4-round probe). I kept a scalar tail path for the remainder even though `256*1024` is divisible by 8.\n\n`c_src/` was not modified (`git status` on it is clean). My scratch build directory has been removed; the leftover hidden `.scratch/.claude` entries are sandbox-injected bind mounts I can't unmount, containing none of my files."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":534,"cache_read_input_tokens":42340,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":534,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"57d22922-0ba4-40ee-ba0b-b670be698e43","uuid":"8f018212-1c40-4aa8-b6d1-7804a641830e","timestamp":"2026-08-28T10:55:17.290Z"}
